#{[17592186514414 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514414 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514414 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514414 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is **not** a set?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514414 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68a9ef69-1a68-5a42-bd02-0ed93671db30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514414 #edb/ref 1074 "A set is a collection of elements listed between two braces (curly brackets). However, note that the null set (the set that does not contain any elements) can be written as either $\\{\\}$ or $∅.$\n\n- $\\frac{2}{7},\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* a set. The numbers $\\frac{2}{7},$ $\\frac{2}{5},$ $\\frac{2}{3},$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$ are *not* listed between two braces.\n\nAll the other choices are sets.\n\n- $\\{\\frac{2}{7},\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}\\}$ is a set. The elements are $\\frac{2}{7},\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}$ and they are listed between two braces.\n\n- $\\{\\{\\}\\}$ is a set. It contains a single element, $\\{\\},$ and this element is between two braces.\n\n- $\\{\\}$ is a set. It is the null set.\n\n- $∅$ is a set. It is the null set." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514419 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514419 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514419 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},$ and ${2}^{9}$ are elements of $B$, but ${3}^{4}$ and ${5}^{0}$ are not, then which of the following is true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ae10c4a-b478-5b9e-9599-7eb7b75dca9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514419 #edb/ref 1074 "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},$ and ${2}^{9}$ are elements of $B,$ we write ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},{2}^{9}\\in B.$\n\n- Since ${3}^{4}$ and ${5}^{0}$ are *not* elements of $B,$ we write ${3}^{4},{5}^{0}\\notin B.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  ${2}^{5},{2}^{7},{2}^{9}\\in B$ and ${3}^{4},{5}^{0}\\notin B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514420 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514420 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514420 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514420 #edb/ref 1071 "If $x,y,$ and $z$ are elements of $B$, but $v$ and $w$ are not, then which of the following is true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b96bbb9-30d0-511e-a26c-5b394ffc1b3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514420 #edb/ref 1074 "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since $x,y,$ and $z$ are elements of $B,$ we write $x,y,z\\in B.$\n\n- Since $v$ and $w$ are *not* elements of $B,$ we write $v,w\\notin B.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  $x,y,z\\in B$ and $v,w\\notin B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514425 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514425 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104952" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514425 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514425 #edb/ref 1071 "Which set represents all integers divisible by $5$ from $-21$ to $11?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514425 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76066cd5-d606-50ce-8894-909f30c9f6a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514425 #edb/ref 1074 "To express the desired set, we start with the first integer divisible by $5$ greater than or equal to $-21,$ which is $-20.$ The next integer divisible by $5$ is $-15,$ the one after that is $-10,$ and so on up to $10$ (which is the last number that is divisible by $5$ and less than or equal to $11$).\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{-20,-15,-10,…,10\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514460 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514460 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514460 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514460 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is an element of the set $A=\\{♢,\\,10,\\,a,\\,◻\\}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514460 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf7a3bf9-7841-5c39-8953-649f06640b22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514460 #edb/ref 1074 "The elements of our set are as follows:\n\n$$\n♢,\\,10,\\,a,\\,◻\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, the only element that belongs to the set is $◻.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514474 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514474 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80286" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514474 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514474 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\pi ,\\text{e}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ are elements of $Y$, but $0$ and $1$ are not, then which of the following is true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514474 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f10fdf14-c3b0-5ff7-a1e1-67a02a7a3852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514474 #edb/ref 1074 "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since $\\pi ,\\text{e}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ are elements of $Y,$ we write $\\pi ,\\text{e},\\sqrt{2}\\in Y.$\n\n- Since $0$ and $1$ are *not* elements of $Y,$ we write $0,1\\notin Y.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  $\\pi ,\\text{e},\\sqrt{2}\\in Y$ and $0,1\\notin Y$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514475 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514475 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514475 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514475 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is **not** a set?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514475 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2f32264-6492-5e60-8c09-298d7626357d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514475 #edb/ref 1074 "A set is a collection of elements listed between two braces (curly brackets). However, note that the null set (the set that does not contain any elements) can be written as either $\\{\\}$ or $∅.$\n\n- $f,g,h$ is *not* a set. The letters $f,$ $g,$ and $h$ are *not* listed between two braces.\n\nAll the other choices are sets.\n\n- $\\{f,\\{h\\}\\}$ is a set. The elements are $f,$ and $\\{h\\},$ and they are listed between the two outermost braces.\n\n- $\\{∅,\\{f,g\\}\\}$ is a set. The elements are $∅,$ and $\\{f,g\\},$ and are listed between the two outermost braces.\n\n- $\\{\\}$ is a set. It is the null set.\n\n- $\\{1,f,2,g\\}$ is a set. The elements are $1,$ $f,$ $2,$ and $g,$ and they are listed between two braces." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
